// Practice quiz: Semantic HTML page structure
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/html-and-css/semantic-html-page-structure

::o1 product reviews::A shop page lists customer reviews. Each review has its own author, date and text and would still make sense if shown alone in a feed. Which element should wrap each review? {
=article #Correct. An article is a self-contained item that could be reused on its own, such as a comment or a review.
~section #Not quite. A section is a themed group of content; a self-contained item that stands alone is an article.
~aside #Not quite. An aside holds content only tangentially related to what is around it; the reviews are the content.
~div #Not quite. A div carries no meaning, so assistive technology learns nothing about the review.
}

::o1 one main::How many visible main elements should a page have? {
=Exactly one, wrapping the content unique to that page #Correct. HTML allows only one main element without the hidden attribute, and it holds the dominant content of the page.
~One for every article on the page #Not quite. Articles sit inside the one main; main is not repeated per item.
~None, because the body element already marks the main content #Not quite. The body holds everything, including the header and footer; main marks only the dominant content.
~As many as there are columns in the layout #Not quite. Columns are a CSS layout matter; main is about meaning, not position.
}

::o2 skipped level::A page has the headings h1 Garden guide, h2 Vegetables, then h4 Tomatoes. What is wrong? {
=Going down from h2 to h4 skips level 3, so Tomatoes should be an h3 #Correct. Each heading may be at most one level deeper than the heading before it.
~Nothing, because h4 makes the Tomatoes heading smaller #Not quite. The number is a level in the outline, not a font size; CSS sets the size.
~A page may not use more than three heading levels #Not quite. HTML has six levels; the problem is the skip, not the depth.
~Vegetables should also be an h1 #Not quite. Vegetables belongs under Garden guide, so h2 is right; the skip is after it.
}

::o2 going back up::A page goes h1, h2, h3, h4 and then the next heading starts a new topic at the same rank as the first h2. Which level should that heading use? {
=h2, because jumping back up any number of levels is allowed #Correct. Only going down is limited to one level at a time; going up may jump several levels.
~h5, because headings must keep going down #Not quite. Headings follow the outline, so a new top-level topic returns to h2.
~h3, because a heading may change by only one level in either direction #Not quite. The one-level limit applies only when going deeper.
~h1, because every new topic needs its own h1 #Not quite. The new topic is a sibling of the first h2, so it is an h2.
}

::o3 keep the layout::The wrapper div class site-top holds the logo and site name. You change it to header class site-top. The CSS rule .site-top sets display flex. What happens to the layout? {
=Nothing changes, because the class still matches and header displays as a block like div #Correct. The selector still matches the class, and both elements display as blocks by default before your CSS applies.
~The layout breaks, because CSS classes only work on div elements #Not quite. A class selector matches any element with that class.
~The logo and name stack, because header ignores display flex #Not quite. CSS applies to header exactly as it does to div.
~The page gains a new visual style from header #Not quite. Semantic elements add meaning, not a visible style.
}
